00:04:51.500 --> 00:04:57.000
Now here is something that most retail investors get wrong and it cost me money before I figured it

00:04:57.000 --> 00:05:04.260
out. Imagine you bought an ETF tracking Japanese equities last week when the Nikkei 225 was already

00:05:04.260 --> 00:05:10.680
sliding. Your order filled at the ask but the quoted spread had just widened from 4 cents to

00:05:10.680 --> 00:05:17.400
18 cents in the first 20 minutes of trading. The mechanism at work is liquidity withdrawal by market

00:05:17.400 --> 00:05:24.120
makers when overnight news such as rate worries and regional equity weakness increases perceived risk.

00:05:24.980 --> 00:05:31.120
The displayed bid drops faster than the ask can be hit. But most retail investors do not realize

00:05:31.120 --> 00:05:37.480
is that the effective spread you actually pay can be 2-3 times the displayed quote once you factor

00:05:37.480 --> 00:05:43.200
in the depth at each level. Checking level 2 data before market open reveals whether the inside

00:05:43.200 --> 00:05:49.400
market is one sided. The professionals always compare the current spread to the 20 day average before

00:05:49.400 --> 00:05:55.680
entering. If it is more than 50% wider, they either wait or use a limit order at the midpoint.

00:05:56.320 --> 00:06:01.220
The biggest mistake with spread dynamics is assuming the posted quote is the cost you will pay.

00:06:01.820 --> 00:06:07.540
Instead, always pull up the time and sales tape for the prior 15 minutes and size your order to

00:06:07.540 --> 00:06:13.820
know more than 20% of the visible depth at the bid. Tools that quantify hidden risks are becoming

